    "title": "Somalia Signs AfCFTA Agreement Into Law, Final Step to Join Trade Bloc",
    "original_title": "Somalia clears final legal hurdle to join Africa&#8217;s free trade bloc",
    "slug": "somalia-signs-afcfta-agreement-into-law-final-step-to-join-trade-bloc",
    "summary": "President Hassan Sheikh Mohamud has signed the African Continental Free Trade Area agreement, completing Somalia's final legal requirement for membership. Economists note that boosting domestic production and exports will be key to benefiting from the bloc.",
    "content": "Somalia has taken the final legal step toward joining the African Continental Free Trade Area (AfCFTA) after President Hassan Sheikh Mohamud signed the agreement into law. The signing, which occurred this week, fulfills the last domestic requirement for Somalia's formal accession to the continent-wide trade bloc, which aims to create a single market for goods and services across 54 African nations.\n\nThe AfCFTA, established in 2018, is the largest free trade area in the world by participating countries, with a combined GDP of over $3 trillion. Somalia's entry has been anticipated since it submitted its instrument of ratification in 2023, but the process was delayed by the need for parliamentary approval and presidential assent. The signing now clears the path for Somalia to formally deposit its ratification with the African Union, making it an official member.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this development carries significant implications. The diaspora, which sends billions of dollars in remittances annually, has long been a key driver of local investment, particularly in trade and services. Joining the AfCFTA could lower trade barriers, making it easier for diaspora-owned businesses to export goods to other African markets and to import inputs at lower costs. However, economists caution that the primary challenge for Somalia is not access to markets but the capacity to produce competitively.\n\nSomalia's economy remains heavily reliant on livestock, agriculture, and remittances, with limited manufacturing and export diversification. Infrastructure deficits, weak institutions, and ongoing security concerns continue to hinder production growth. As one analyst noted, \"The agreement is a welcome step, but without improving productivity and export capacity, Somalia may struggle to reap the benefits of the free trade area.\"\n\nThe government has expressed optimism that the AfCFTA membership will attract foreign investment and stimulate local industries. In a statement, officials highlighted plans to develop export zones and improve trade facilitation. However, observers point out that tangible gains will require coordinated efforts to upgrade logistics, reduce corruption, and strengthen the private sector.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, the news is a reminder of the country's gradual reintegration into continental institutions. It also signals potential new opportunities for trade and business partnerships between Somalia and other African countries. As the country moves forward, the diaspora will likely play a crucial role in leveraging these opportunities, given their experience in global markets and their investment in Somalia's future.\n\nRead the original report from Horseed Media here: https://horseedmedia.net/somalia-clears-final-legal-hurdle-to-join-africas-free-trade-bloc/424326/",
    "source": "horseedmedia.net",
    "source_name": "Horseed Media",
